 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Structure",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Two full bridges, A and B, each made of four bipolar output transistors driven by AND gates. In each bridge the emitters of the two lower transistors are joined and brought out on a sense pin, so an external resistor there measures that bridge's load current. The logic has its own supply pin, VSS, and its own reference, so it runs at a lower voltage than the output stage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inputs and enables",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Each bridge has two inputs and an enable: IN1, IN2 and EN_A for bridge A, IN3, IN4 and EN_B for bridge B. With the enable high, each input sets its output high or low; with the enable low the whole bridge is off. All inputs accept TTL levels, and a logic low is recognised up to 1.5 V. Each input should be wired to its signal source over a very short path."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Load connection",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Depending on its inputs, a bridge drives an inductive load in common or differential mode. The load current returns through that bridge's sense pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power sequencing",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The enable inputs are to be held low before VS is switched on and before it is switched off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Decoupling and grounding",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "VS and VSS each get a non-inductive capacitor to ground, usually 100 nF, placed as close as possible to the GND pin. When the supply's bulk capacitor is far from the chip, a second, smaller capacitor goes near the L298. The sense resistor must not be wire-wound, and it is grounded close to the negative terminal of VS, which in turn is close to the chip's GND pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current sensing and protection",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The voltage on the sense resistor lets the load current be regulated by switching the inputs on and off, or lets the part be protected against overcurrent by pulling the enable low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Clamp diodes",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Inductive loads and chopped inputs require an external diode bridge on the outputs. The diodes are fast recovery parts, trr ≤ 200 ns, with as low a forward voltage as possible at the worst-case load current; Schottky diodes are preferred."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Braking",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "During a fast motor stop (braking) the current must stay within the 2 A absolute maximum."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Paralleling",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "For repetitive peak currents above 2 A the two bridges can be paralleled: outputs 1 and 4 together and outputs 2 and 3 together. Paralleled, the part carries up to 3 A DC and 3.5 A repetitive peak."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching times",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Source and sink delays are measured from the 50 % point of the input or enable edge (a 4 V swing) to the 90 % or 10 % point of a 2 A load current; fall and rise times run between the 90 % and 10 % points."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overtemperature",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The part has overtemperature protection."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal tab",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The Multiwatt15 tab is connected to pin 8, GND."
  }
 ],
